CAT = "cparegCreditors"; SEG = "CRE"
Q = [
("Under UCC Article 9, a security interest attaches when value is given, the debtor has rights in the collateral, and:",
 "There is a security agreement", ["A financing statement is filed", "The debtor is notified in writing", "The debt becomes past due"],
 "Attachment needs value, the debtor's rights in the collateral and an authenticated security agreement (or the creditor's possession). Filing is a way to perfect, not to attach."),
("A store sells a TV on credit to a consumer for home use and keeps a purchase-money security interest. The interest is perfected:",
 "Automatically on attachment", ["Only on filing", "When the store keeps the TV", "Twenty days after the sale"],
 "A purchase-money security interest in consumer goods is perfected automatically when it attaches, with no filing needed."),
("A buyer in the ordinary course of business buys inventory from a dealer whose lender has a perfected security interest in it. The buyer takes the goods:",
 "Free of the security interest", ["Subject to the security interest", "Subject to it if they knew of it", "Free only after paying the lender"],
 "A buyer in the ordinary course takes free of a security interest created by the seller, even a perfected one the buyer knows about."),
("Two lenders take security interests in the same equipment. Lender A files first; Lender B's interest attaches first. Both are perfected. Who has priority?",
 "Lender A, the first to file", ["Lender B, the first to attach", "They share it pro rata", "Whoever lent money first"],
 "Between perfected security interests, priority goes to the first to file or perfect. Attachment order does not decide priority."),
("A surety pays the creditor in full after the debtor defaults. The surety's right to take over the creditor's rights against the debtor is:",
 "Subrogation", ["Exoneration", "Contribution", "Reimbursement"],
 "Subrogation puts the paying surety in the creditor's shoes, including any collateral. Reimbursement is the separate right to be repaid by the debtor."),
("Two co-sureties each guaranteed the same loan in full. One pays the whole debt. Her right to collect a share from the other is:",
 "Contribution", ["Subrogation", "Exoneration", "Indemnity"],
 "Contribution lets a co-surety who pays more than her share recover the excess from the other co-sureties, in proportion to their guarantees."),
("A debtor, insolvent, pays an unsecured, unrelated creditor on an old debt 60 days before filing Chapter 7. The trustee can generally:",
 "Recover it as a preference", ["Not recover a paid debt", "Recover it only on proof of fraud", "Recover it only after a year"],
 "Transfers to an unsecured creditor for an old debt within 90 days of filing while insolvent are avoidable preferences. The period is one year for insiders."),
("In a Chapter 7 case, which unsecured claim has the highest priority?",
 "Domestic support obligations", ["Employee wage claims", "Recent income taxes", "General trade creditors"],
 "Since the 2005 Act, domestic support obligations rank first among priority unsecured claims, ahead of wages and taxes. General unsecured creditors share what is left."),
("Which debt is generally NOT discharged in a Chapter 7 bankruptcy?",
 "Unpaid child support", ["Credit card balances", "Unpaid medical bills", "Unsecured personal loans"],
 "Domestic support obligations such as child support are never discharged. Ordinary consumer debts like credit cards and medical bills are."),
("Under the IRS common law test, behavioral control looks at whether the business controls:",
 "How the work is done", ["Who buys the materials", "Whether the worker profits", "How long the job lasts"],
 "Behavioral control asks whether the business directs how, when and where the work is done. Who pays for materials and the chance of profit are financial control factors."),
("Federal unemployment (FUTA) tax is paid by:",
 "The employer only", ["The employee only", "Employer and employee equally", "The state agency"],
 "FUTA is an employer-only tax; nothing is withheld from wages. FICA, by contrast, is shared by employer and employee."),
("Under the anti-bribery rules of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act, which payment to a foreign official is allowed?",
 "A facilitating payment", ["A gift to win a contract", "A bribe paid through an agent", "A payment to keep a contract"],
 "The FCPA excepts small facilitating payments for routine government action, such as processing papers. Payments to obtain or retain business are banned, directly or through agents."),
]
